ENA recovery regression

Compiles the driver's reset task, device recovery function and admin
interrupt handler from sys/dev/pci/if_ena.c against a small model of the
ena-com HAL whose admin queue has stopped completing commands, then runs
eight cases: a timed-out admin queue is rebuilt before I/O restarts;
repeated recovery; an admin queue already marked dead; a failed hardware
reset retains its DMA memory and permits a retry; a failed admin
initialization stays interrupt-safe until a retry; a changed device
identity is rejected; a stale reset task leaves a stopped interface alone;
a masked admin handler never touches destroyed rings.

	make regress

or, from the top of the tree,

	sh regress/sys/dev/ena/reset.sh sys/dev/pci/if_ena.c

The failure the model reproduces is a keep-alive watchdog timeout whose
DESTROY_SQ then times out, after which CREATE_CQ returns ENODEV and the
interface stays down until the host reboots.  The model emulates no DMA,
MMIO or MSI-X; it needs no ENA hardware.

ENA keep-alive watchdog regression

watchdog.sh compiles ena_admin_init() and ena_tick() from the driver
against a model with configurable device features and a fake clock.  It
checks keep-alive negotiation, default and device-supplied deadlines,
deadline boundaries, disabled keep-alive monitoring, and renegotiation
after reset.  The TX-progress watchdog must still detect stalled queues
when keep-alive monitoring is disabled.

	sh regress/sys/dev/ena/watchdog.sh sys/dev/pci/if_ena.c

The model tests watchdog policy, not DMA, interrupt delivery or hardware
reset ordering.
